Nuclear star clusters (SCBs) are dense collections of stars located at the centers of many galaxies, including those with supermassive black holes that have been ejected after stellar interactions. The accumulation of black holes (SMBBs) in these populated areas can have far-reaching ramifications. For example, their gravitational influence might strip away the cores of galaxies or disrupt the accretion required for supermassive black hole (SMBHs). Disruptions in these populations also produce gravitational radiation detectable by LIGO and Virgo.

However, the study of these groups remains difficult due to the inherent model uncertainty. The current analysis employs Monte Carlo methods to examine nucleosynthesis, stellar evolution, and the gravitational binding of objects to test the clusters against existing observations and assess their relevance as a source of gravitational radiation against other known clusters. Furthermore, the research establishes clear criteria for the presence of an embedded object relative to upcoming detectability windows of LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA. By applying these criteria to LIRGS (infrared bright galaxies), it is predicted that every nucleated cluster contains at least one object; depleting the central region of star-forming material.

